  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1109 AM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nAll focus initially directed shortwave trough/upper level speed max \nand attendant rather weak surface reflection dropping southeast into \nthe region within broad upper level northwest flow...part of \nremarkably persistent Intermountain West/High Plains centered upper \nlevel ridge/heat dome and northeast NOAM long wave troughing \nconfiguration. This wave will continue to kick off showers and \nthunderstorms across the region this afternoon into early this \nevening. Large area of Canada originated high pressure set to follow \nthrough the mid-week period. While current NOAM upper level flow \nregime will wax and wane at times into the weekend, overall \nconfiguration will remain...continuing the northwest flow aloft.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nNearly all focus directed at convective evolution into this evening. \nSecondary emphasis on beach hazard concerns Tuesday and \ntemperature/additional precipitation trends through the remainder of \nthe period.\n\nDetails: \n\nMature MCS tied to above mentioned wave racing southeast across\neastern Wisconsin...simply following axis of intense \ninstability gradient and pre-existing differential heating\nboundary. Western periphery of this activity pushing into \nnorthern Lake Michigan, but most organized and intense storms \nremain to our west. Expect this storm cluster to continue \nsoutheast into the southern Lakes. Another very impressive \ncomplex noted over portions of central Lake Huron, likely \nputting out some gusty winds and isolated pockets of large hail\nover the big body of water. Radar trends suggest this activity \nwill stay offshore. Do expect some additional showers and storms\nto percolate across our area into this evening as primary upper\nlevel support passes overhead. Could see a few strong to severe\nstorms, capable of producing some gusty winds and large hail. \nAgain, best course of action is to continue to monitor radar \ntrends into this evening. \n\nRain threat ends overnight as high pressure builds southeast into \nthe northern Lakes. Dry and seasonably mild Tuesday, with marginally \ngusty northwest winds within tight pressure gradient between eastern \nLakes low and the southeast diving high pressure. These winds will \nallow for some decent wave development, resulting in hazardous \nswimming conditions on at least some of our beaches of northern Lake \nMichigan. High pressure drops directly overhead Tuesday night into \nThursday. Could see some decently chilly early morning temperatures \nWednesday and Thursday...when some of our traditional colder \ninterior areas could make a run into the 40s. Airmass modification \nwill be substantial by the daytime hours of Thursday, with plenty of \nsunshine helping temperatures recover back into the 80s. Additional \npassing waves may bring some additional shower/storm chances Friday \ninto the start of the weekend. As one can imagine, uncertainty is \nhigh, with plenty of time to work out those details in the coming \nday. Seasonably mild weather to continue through the weekend.",
